<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Democratic Party (United States)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Democratic Party is one of the two major political parties of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, organised in the 1820s around supporters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-jackson">Andrew Jackson</a> and descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a>'s Democratic-Republicans. It contested elections against the Whigs and, from the 1850s, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republican-party-united-states">Republican Party</a>. In the 20th century it became the party of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-deal">New Deal</a> and of civil rights legislation, with strong support among labour unions.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Party politics emerged early, with Federalists against Jeffersonian Republicans, later <b>Democrats</b> against Whigs and, from the 1850s, Democrats against the new Republican Party.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-states">United States · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The ruling Liberal <b>Democratic Party</b> and Komeito supported it; the Social Democratic and Communist parties opposed it, citing the symbols' links with the war era.</blockquote><a href="/songs/kimigayo">Kimigayo</a></li><li><blockquote>Other unions, among them the AFL–CIO, adopted it, and it is still sung at union meetings, protests and rallies in the United States, Australia, New Zealand and Canada, and at conferences of the Australian Labor Party and Canada's New <b>Democratic Party</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/solidarity-forever">Solidarity Forever</a></li><li><blockquote>The donkey later became the accepted symbol of the <b>Democratic Party</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-15">15 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Nisei soldiers of the 442nd Regimental Combat Team won fame in Europe, and in 1954 <b>Democrats</b> backed by labour unions ended Republican dominance.</blockquote><a href="/state/territory-of-hawaii">Territory of Hawaii</a></li></ul></section>
